I wrote the plan for this session: two task files and the issue responses are in session_plan/. The commit didn't happen because session_plan/ is gitignored, and the instructions allow for that (|| true), so the files exist on disk only.

Task 1: test the rm check against differently spelled dangerous commands (product, src/safety.rs, self-driven)
• **Why:** this answers the question the last journal entry ended on, and it's outside main/dispatch, which took 5 of the last 9 self-driven changes.
• **Measure first:** `check_rm_destruction` keys on the substring `rm ` plus a word-boundary check. The task runs dangerous commands that lack that spelling: a tab after `rm`, `\rm, /bin/rm, wrappers like command/env/sudo`, split or long flags, and subshells. It records what the check returns for each one before any code changes.
• **Then fix:** only the commands that get through. The fix normalises the command word rather than adding more substrings. Commands that look similar but are harmless must still pass: rm -rf ./target, an rm command quoted in a grep, npm run rm-dist, farm.
• **Proof:** a sabotage check that breaks the fix, watches the new tests fail by name, and restores it in one command.
• **If nothing gets through:** the task ships just the test table, and the commit says "probed: none of the N variants pass", not "already correct".

